import{G as e,W as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.CzKPlK1V.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"LangControl Middleware","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"03-developer/middleware/lang-control.md","filePath":"03-developer/middleware/lang-control.md"}`),a={name:`03-developer/middleware/lang-control.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="langcontrol-middleware" tabindex="-1">LangControl Middleware <a class="header-anchor" href="#langcontrol-middleware" aria-label="Permalink to “LangControl Middleware”">​</a></h1><p>This Middleware manages the language for the website&#39;s visitor-facing sections. Its purpose is to determine which language to use when processing a request so that page content, translations, and language-dependent information are loaded correctly. The language can be specified by the first segment of the page URL; otherwise, the default language defined in the application settings is used.</p><p>In this structure, the language can appear at the beginning of the URL as a two-letter code. For example, <code>/fa/articles</code> indicates a request for the articles page in the <code>fa</code> language. The Middleware first checks the initial URL segment and, if it consists of two English letters, checks for a corresponding translation file in <code>resources/lang</code>. The request language is considered valid, and the application language is changed, only if the corresponding translation file exists.</p><p>After determining the language, its value is also saved in the user&#39;s session. This makes the selected language available to other parts of the application and keeps the request&#39;s language information accessible for subsequent processing. A property named <code>set_lang</code> is also added to the request attributes so that other parts of the application can use it to determine whether the language was specified through the URL. This property is only a request-level marker and does not, by itself, change the behavior of other parts of the application; its use depends on how the other components are implemented.</p><p>If the URL begins with a language code but the corresponding translation file does not exist, the request is redirected to the visitor-facing welcome route. Conversely, if the URL does not begin with a two-letter language code, the Middleware retrieves and applies the application&#39;s default language from the <code>app.locale</code> setting. Therefore, the request language is determined by the URL structure and the existence of a translation file; if the language is not specified in the URL, the application falls back to its default language.</p><p>Alongside the application&#39;s content translation system, including the <code>spatie/laravel-translatable</code> package, this Middleware plays an important role in determining the language used to display information. The <code>spatie/laravel-translatable</code> package provides a way to store and retrieve multilingual translations for model fields. Therefore, correctly setting the application language before processing a request is important so that components dependent on the current language receive information in the expected language. However, this Middleware does not translate model fields itself; its responsibility is limited to setting the current language and providing the conditions needed to process translations correctly.</p><p>Overall, <code>LangControl</code> is a point in the request processing flow where the language for visitor-facing pages is determined based on the URL or the application&#39;s default settings. This mechanism helps maintain consistency in the display of multilingual content and, alongside the model translation system, provides the conditions for loading information in the selected language.</p>`,7)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};